THE CASE FOR FAIL:
The suspension-stability axiom directly conflicts with reinstatement as a derived property, which the problem requires be preserved. Consider a graph where node x is undecided because its sole defense path is length k+1. The axiom forbids any bounded-depth support chain addition from flipping x to accepted unless x already had a defense path of length ≤ k. But reinstatement legitimately allows a previously undecided node to become accepted after arguments are added that defeat its attackers. The axiom blocks exactly those legitimate reinstatement scenarios where the new support chain is itself the mechanism by which x gains a valid defense, making reinstatement no longer derivable for that class of graphs.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's case conflates two structurally distinct scenarios and, in doing so, misattributes the axiom's effect.

(1) The scenario described — 'node x is undecided because its sole defense path is length k+1' — is internally inconsistent with standard grounded semantics. In grounded semantics, a node is undecided (neither credulously accepted nor definitively rejected) when its status cannot be resolved by iterative defense propagation, not merely because its shortest defense path exceeds some depth bound k. If x genuinely has a complete, attack-free defense path of any finite length, grounded evaluation would accept x once that path is activated. Undecidedness arises from unbroken attack cycles or unattacked attackers, not from path length alone. The critic has not established that the described graph actually exists under grounded semantics.

(2) Reinstatement, as a derived property, concerns nodes that were accepted, then defeated (becoming undecided or rejected), then restored to acceptance when their attackers are defeated. The classic pattern is: x accepted → a attacks x → b attacks a → x reinstated. In this pattern, x possesses a defense path (x ← b ← a, or simply x defended by b against a) that becomes valid once b is introduced. The axiom permits exactly this: if x already has a defense path of length ≤ k in the original graph (the pre-addition state where x is undecided), then adding the support chain that activates that path can flip x to accepted. The axiom does not block reinstatement where the defense path already structurally exists.

(3) The critic's claimed conflict arises only in the sub-case where 'the new support chain is itself the mechanism by which x gains a valid defense' — meaning x had NO defense path of length ≤ k before the addition. But this is not reinstatement of a previously accepted node; it is creation of a novel defense. Reinstatement requires that x was previously accepted, which presupposes x had a valid defense path before being attacked. A node that was never accepted and had no defense path cannot be 'reinstated' — it can only be newly accepted. The critic has not established that this scenario falls under legitimate reinstatement rather than ordinary incremental argumentation.

(4) The axiom is specifically scoped to 'bounded-depth support chain' additions, not to all argument additions. Standard reinstatement operates by defeating attackers, which is an attack-edge addition, not a support-chain addition. The axiom does not constrain attack-edge additions that defeat x's attackers, so the core reinstatement mechanism remains fully derivable.

The critic has not established that the axiom blocks any scenario that is both (a) legitimately classifiable as reinstatement under standard semantics and (b) realizable under grounded semantics as described. The case rests on an equivocation between 'newly created defense' and 'reinstatement of prior acceptance.'

